  "solution": {
    "text": "Impurities affect surface tension appreciably. It is observed that impurities which tend to concentrate on surface of liquids, compared to its bulk lower the surface tension.<br /><br />Substances like detergents and soaps <math> <mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"> <mrow> <msub> <mrow> <mtext>CH</mtext></mrow> <mn>3</mn> </msub> <msub> <mrow> <mfenced> <mrow> <msub> <mrow> <mtext>CH</mtext></mrow> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfenced></mrow> <mrow> <mn>11</mn></mrow> </msub> <msubsup> <mrow> <mtext>SO</mtext></mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mo>−</mo> </msubsup> <msup> <mrow> <mtext>Na</mtext></mrow> <mo>+</mo> </msup> </mrow> </mfenced> </math> decrease the surface tension sharply.<br /><br />Those like alcohol (eg. <math><mtext>C</mtext><msub><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mtext>OH</mtext><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mtext>OH</mtext></math> ) lower the surface tension slightly.<br /><br />Inorganic impurities present in bulk of a liquid such as <math> <mrow> <mtext>KCl</mtext></mrow> </math> tend to increase the surface tension of water."
